<file path=".codex/prompts/generate-code.md">
---
description: Run build_runner, localization, and Firebase generators with validation
argument-hint: [ SCOPE=all|build|localization|firebase|custom ] [ CLEAN=true|false ]
---
You are Codex operating inside the Meup Flutter repo. Execute every generator that matters for this project and return a validation report.

## Inputs
- If `SCOPE` is missing, ask whether to run `all` (default), `build`, `localization`, `firebase`, or a comma-separated custom subset.
- If `CLEAN` is missing, ask if `dart run build_runner clean` should run first (default `true` when SCOPE contains `build`).
- Clarify any blockers (e.g., Firebase auth) before running commands.

## Workflow
1. Run `flutter pub get`.
2. When `CLEAN` is `true`, run `dart run build_runner clean` and note removed artifacts.
3. When scope includes `build`, run `dart run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs`. Surface any missing `part` directives or generation errors with file paths.
4. When scope includes `localization`, run `./tools/macos/localization/generate_localization.sh` followed by `flutter gen-l10n`. Confirm ARB files and generated Dart files updated under `lib/config/l10n/`.
5. When scope includes `firebase`, run `./tools/macos/config/flutterfire_config.sh` for every flavor. Warn if Firebase CLI authentication is required.
6. After generators finish, always run `flutter analyze` and highlight any new issues.

## Validation & Reporting
- Summarize which generators ran, how long they took, and whether they succeeded.
- Mention the count of regenerated files when possible (e.g., `*.freezed.dart`, localization files, Firebase option files).
- Call out failures with actionable fixes (conflicting outputs, missing part directives, Google Sheets or Firebase auth errors).
- Close with next-step suggestions if further manual work is needed.
</file>

<file path=".codex/prompts/new-pull-request.md">
---
description: Analyze git changes and draft a Clean Architecture aware PR description
argument-hint: [ TARGET_BRANCH=develop|main|<name> ] [ INCLUDE_SHOTS=true|false ]
---
You are Codex preparing a pull request summary for the Meup Flutter app. Produce a high-signal PR description that classifies every change across architecture layers and flags risk.

## Inputs
- If `TARGET_BRANCH` is absent, ask which branch to diff against (default `develop`).
- If `INCLUDE_SHOTS` is absent, ask whether screenshots or recordings are available; mention them only when the user confirms.
- Confirm the working tree is clean aside from intentional changes; ask the user to stash/commit otherwise.

## Workflow
1. Collect git data: current branch, diff vs `TARGET_BRANCH`, stats, per-file lists, commit subjects, and `pubspec.yaml` diff for dependency changes.
2. Derive ticket IDs from the branch name (`MEUP-###` or similar). Ask the user if none can be inferred.
3. Categorize changes:
   - Map files to architecture layers (Domain/Data/Presentation/Config/Assets/Tests/Docs/Build).
   - Group commits by intent (features, improvements, fixes, tests, docs, tooling).
   - Detect breaking interface changes (e.g., repository signatures, removed entity fields, route updates).
   - Detect dependency additions or version bumps from `pubspec.yaml`.
4. Summarize impact: highlight high/medium/low risk areas, new dependencies, migrations, or follow-up work.
5. Draft the PR content in Markdown with sections:
   - Summary (2–3 sentences).
   - Ticket / Branch / Target.
   - Change statistics (files, additions, deletions, commit count).
   - Layer-by-layer breakdown with concise bullet points.
   - Breaking changes / migrations (if any).
   - New dependencies or tooling updates.
   - Testing evidence (list the commands/results actually run).
   - Screenshots/media (only when `INCLUDE_SHOTS=true` and assets are available).

## Validation & Reporting
- Quote concrete file paths and commit SHAs where helpful.
- Call out gaps: missing tests, flaky analysis, or items needing reviewer focus.
- Close with clear next steps (e.g., “run flutter test” if unverified).
</file>

<file path=".codex/prompts/new-release.md">
---
description: Cut a release branch, generate notes, and tag the version automatically
argument-hint: [ STATUS=create|update ] [ BASE_BRANCH=develop|release/<ver> ]
---
You are Codex automating the Meup release workflow. Produce a release branch (or refresh an existing one), generate categorized notes, and create the git tag once validation passes.

## Inputs
- If `STATUS` is missing, ask whether we are creating a new release from `develop` or updating the current `release/*` branch.
- Confirm the version declared in `pubspec.yaml` and show it to the user for approval before proceeding.
- Ensure the working tree is clean; stop and ask the user if uncommitted files exist.

## Workflow
1. Capture context: current branch, version, latest tags, pending commits vs `origin/main`, and release history.
2. When `STATUS=create`, checkout `develop` and create `release/<version>`; when `STATUS=update`, stay on the release branch.
3. Analyze all commits since the previous tag (normally `origin/main`..`HEAD`):
   - Compute stats (files changed, additions, deletions, commit count).
   - Group commits into Features, Improvements, Fixes, Platform, Dependencies, Tooling using commit prefixes and file paths.
   - Flag breaking changes (e.g., repository interface shifts, migrations) and dependency updates from `pubspec.yaml`.
4. Draft release notes in Markdown:
   - Title `Release Notes - Meup v<version>`.
   - Sections for 🎉 Features, 🔧 Improvements, 🐛 Fixes, 📱 Platform (iOS/Android), 🔄 Dependencies & Tools, 📊 Statistics, Known Issues/Follow-ups.
   - Provide concrete highlights (entities touched, new APIs, performance wins). Reference files when possible.
5. Tagging:
   - Create an annotated tag `v<version>` with a concise summary plus a link to release notes.
   - Push the branch and tag (`git push origin release/<version>` and `git push origin v<version>`).
6. Share validation steps (tests, analyze, build scripts) that ran during the process or flag remaining work.

## Validation & Reporting
- Stop immediately if the branch or tag already exists; prompt the user for confirmation.
- Mention any blockers (CI failures, dependency sync issues) and how to resolve them.
- Output the final release note snippet plus the commands executed so reviewers can reproduce.
</file>

<file path=".codex/prompts/setup-new-feature.md">
---
description: Scaffold a full Clean Architecture feature (domain, data, presentation skeleton)
argument-hint: [ FEATURE=<snake_case> ] [ DATA_SOURCE=rest|graphql|local|none ] [ CUBIT=true|false ]
---
You are Codex building a brand-new feature inside `lib/src/` following Meup’s Clean Architecture patterns.

## Inputs
- If `FEATURE` is missing, ask for the canonical snake_case feature name (e.g., `user_notifications`).
- If `DATA_SOURCE` is missing, ask whether the feature talks to REST, GraphQL, local storage only, or is domain-only.
- If `CUBIT` is missing, ask whether state management scaffolds should be created (HydratedCubit by default).
- Ask for functional requirements: API specs, GraphQL operations, or reference features to mirror. Capture any unique constraints.

## Workflow
1. Explore existing code for analogous features (same data source or UX). Note DI usage, mixins, naming, and file layout. Reference `CLAUDE.md` when needed.
2. Domain layer (`lib/src/<feature>/domain/`):
   - Create `entities/<feature>_<entity>.dart`, update `entities.dart` part directives.
   - Define repository interfaces under `repositories/` returning `Either<AppException, T>`.
   - Generate use cases with params/value objects under `usecases/` and register them with `@lazySingleton`.
3. Data layer (`lib/src/<feature>/data/`):
   - Freezed models with JSON serialization under `models/` plus `toEntity()` extensions.
   - Repository implementations using `GraphQLRequestHandlerMixin` or `RestfulRequestHandlerMixin` depending on `DATA_SOURCE`.
   - Retrofit APIs (`datasources/apis`) or `.graphql` templates plus generated classes when required.
   - Wire repositories to interfaces via `@LazySingleton(as: IFeatureRepository)`.
4. Presentation scaffolding (only when `CUBIT=true`):
   - Create Cubit/State pair under `presentation/cubits/`.
   - Provide starter page/view files or widgets if requirements include UI hooks. Keep UI minimal; this command focuses on logic skeletons.
5. Tests:
   - Drop placeholder unit test files for entities, use cases, repositories, and cubits following existing naming conventions in `test/unit/<feature>/`.
6. Run `dart run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs` (and `flutter analyze`) to ensure Freezed/Injectable artifacts are generated.

## Validation & Reporting
- Present the directory tree of created files and highlight important classes with brief responsibilities.
- Surface follow-ups: missing API details, TODOs inside code, next steps for `/update-new-ux-ui`.
- Remind the user to flesh out real logic/tests where placeholders remain.
</file>

<file path=".codex/prompts/sync-localization.md">
---
description: Scan a Dart file/folder for raw strings, add them to the localization sheet via CLI, and regenerate slang outputs.
argument-hint: path=<file-or-folder> [module=<sheet-tab>] [clean=false]
---
You are Codex in the Meup Flutter repo. Given a `path`, remove raw strings by pushing them into the sheet-driven localization flow, then regenerate slang.

## Inputs
- `path` (required): Dart file or folder to scan.
- `module` (optional): Sheet tab (common/auth/home/...); derive from feature folder name when omitted, fallback `common`.
- `clean` (optional): If true, re-run pull after push even if unchanged (default false).

**Common-first rule**: Generic/shared UI copy (e.g., “Next”, “Back”, “Yes”, “No”, “Skip”, “Continue”, “Done”, “OK”, “Cancel”) must live in `assets/i18n/<lang>/common.yaml`. Reuse existing `common` keys instead of creating duplicates in feature modules.

## Workflow
1) Confirm `path` exists and pick `module` (ask once if ambiguous). Default to `common` for generic buttons/labels; only use feature tabs for feature-specific copy.
2) Pull latest sheet data (scope to the module when possible to avoid rewriting other YAML files):
   - `LOCALIZATION_SA_PATH=.info/key/multilang_key.json tools/<os>/localization/localization.sh pull --sheet-gids "<gid>"`
     - Look up the module gid in `.info/app/localization.json` (active.tabs[].gid). If unsure, omit `--sheet-gids` for a full pull.
     - Linux/macOS use `tools/<os>/localization/localization.sh`; Windows uses `tools/windows/localization/localization.bat`.
3) Detect raw strings not already localized:
   - `rg --glob '*.dart' --no-heading --color never -n "\"[^\"]{2,}\"" {path} | rg -v "context\\.text|AppLocale|\\.tr\\(|l10n|Slang"`
   - Deduplicate; skip tests unless user asked.
4) Propose keys:
  - Naming update (no module prefix): use concise lowerCamelCase that describes the text itself, e.g., `welcomeTitle`, `skipButton`, `emailPlaceholder`. Do **not** prepend the module name (`onboardingWelcomeTitle` -> `welcomeTitle`).
   - Always check `assets/i18n/en/common.yaml` for an existing match before adding a new key. If a generic string already exists in `common`, reuse that key and update Dart code to reference it.
  - If you touch a module that still has prefixed keys, migrate them while you are there: rename in all locale YAML files and update Dart references in the scanned `path` to remove the module prefix.
   - Default text: English string found.
   - Other locales: you may leave blank; tools will fill `(VI)/(ZH) <default text>` on push when missing. Keep placeholders `${param}`, avoid double `$`.
5) Write entries into `assets/i18n/en/<module>.yaml` and mirror to other locale files. Keep placeholders `${param}`, avoid double `$`. For plurals/select, use YAML maps (include `zero`/`=0` when needed):
   ```yaml
   itemCount(param=count):
     zero: No items
     one: ${count} item
     other: ${count} items
   ```
6) Push updates (push reads YAML and refreshes `build/localization/records.json`):
   - `tools/<os>/localization/localization.sh push --apply [--sheet-gids "<gid>"]`
   - If you created/renamed tabs, run `tools/<os>/localization/localization.sh cache-gids --ensure-readme` to refresh the gid cache and ensure the README tab exists.
7) Regenerate + verify:
   - `tools/<os>/localization/localization.sh pull [--sheet-gids "<gid>"]` (or when `clean=true` to force sync)
 - `tools/<os>/localization/localization.sh slang` (respects flutter/fvm/puro)
  - Re-scan `path`; optionally `flutter analyze`.
  - Always finish with a `pull` (scoped if used) to confirm the sheet/local data round-trips without diff.

## Notes
- First sheet tab is `README` (instructions only); module tabs hold data. GID cache is stored in `.info/app/localization.json` and refreshed by the tools.
- Partial pulls (`--sheet-gids`) merge into the dataset and only rewrite YAML for the targeted modules; full pulls clean/rebuild all locale YAMLs.
- Do **not** edit generated files (`lib/config/l10n/*.g.dart`); rely on slang.
- Service account path is defined in `.info/app/localization.json`; override via `LOCALIZATION_SA_PATH` if necessary.
- Mention renamed keys (from prefixed to unprefixed) in your summary.

## Reporting
- List new keys added with file references.
- Call out any legacy prefixed keys you migrated and where references were updated.
- Note commands run and any warnings.
- Call out remaining raw strings, placeholder issues, or sheet auth problems.
</file>

